A||||5||FIEROCIOUS||Cam instalation boo boo whats your opinion?||||1||
Z||000000||FIEROCIOUS||06-29-2000||04:16 AM||||When I removed my old cam to install a new one I was an idiot and (forgot) to make a handle to pull the camshaft out, which resulted it the old cam falling on the bearings.<BR>When I inspected the (bearing) (closes to timeing cover) it had a slight scratch in it but didn't look too bad. <P>Now I said bearing singular because I had to return the engine hoist that evening and it had already been a REAL shlty day!<P>In retrespect I should have looked at all of them but I didn't!<P>The engine is in the car but the timeing cover is not on.<P>The cam moves smoothly and feels all right but being the perfectionist compulsive I am, I am worried that I F'd it up! <P>Should I put new bearings in and rent the hoist yet again and disconnect everything yet again or am I just overly worried?<P>If I should change the bearings can I rent a bearing instalation tool? Can I do this with the cradle dropped in my garage? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Taijiguy||06-29-2000||08:14 AM||||I don't know that I'd worry about it *TOO* much. If the engine was upside down when you pulled the cam, then the damage would be on the top of the bearing, and there's no pressure on it there. The closer to the actual bottom of the bearing the scratch is, the more pressure will applied to it; the more static pressure there'll be. (all of this not taking into account all the goofy rotational stresses being applied) Also figure the cam isn't being subject to all the torque and horespower, and is only turning at 1/2 engine speed. The cam is only being subject to the pressure of the compressed valve springs, and then only one or two at a time per pair of bearings.<BR>If you should choose to replace the bearings, I've seen an article on manufacturing a tool to allow bearing removal, which might be torturous of me to mention, since I can't recall where I saw it! Maybe it helps just knowing it's out there. It could have been on a V6 website, or a cam website (yeah, I know, really narrows it down) If I run across it in my travels I'll be sure to pass the info along.<P>Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000002||TOM||06-29-2000||10:54 AM||||Personnally I would not sweat it. If it were mine engine I would go with it. The cam turns smoothly. The bearings are soft. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||06-29-2000||02:02 PM||||Did something similar with a 350 that I was rebuilding, a few years back. <BR>Never gave me a moment's trouble.<BR>I wouldn't sweat it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||06-29-2000||02:35 PM||||It depends on how big the scratch is and how deep. If it wasn't rough or really gouged, it'll probably be ok. Cam bearings don't really carry a great deal of load compared to the crank bearings. If you're using stock valve springs and the mark wasn't too bad, then don't worry about it. The two outer bearings are the ones that get beat up the most when changing out a cam.||||reg||1
Z||000005||FIEROCIOUS||06-29-2000||02:41 PM||||Thanks guys, I've realy been stressed about this! The scratches are on the bottom of the bearing but from the (one) I looked at it was only lightly scratched. It's a crane cam with springs.<P>Thanks for the reasurance it means alot! ||||reg||1
